# Emulator input caveat for future agents:
# On this VBA-RR setup, very short synthetic confirm taps can be logged successfully yet fail to
# advance title/menu screens. Prefer a held confirm/start press in game context when a title/menu
# screen remains unchanged after a tap. We observed Joy1_Start mapped to 'h' and successfully
# advancing the title screen where short 'a' taps did not.
# Directional caveat on this machine:
# VBA-RR's Joy1 D-pad is currently mapped to letter keys u/d/l/r rather than the physical arrow
# keys. Translate cardinal-direction intent into those backend keys in game context so high-level
# logic can keep using up/down/left/right while the emulator still receives the correct input.
# Confirmed Controller 2 mapping on this machine:
# Joypad/Game Boy controls -> Up=u, Down=d, Left=l, Right=r, A=a, B=b, Select=s, Start=h.
# Emulator-only extras -> L=k, R=e, Speed=x, Capture=j, Gameshark=g.
# B/cancel caveat on this machine:
# A short B tap may be logged as successful yet fail to close a menu. A brief held B press
# has worked more reliably for backing out of Pokemon pause/menu states on this VBA-RR setup.
# Keep the GB semantic surface focused on gameplay controls by default; treat the emulator-only
# extras as reserved/opt-in so the agent does not toggle speed/capture/Gameshark accidentally.
# Focus caveat on this machine:
# Foregrounding VBA-RR alone is not always enough. If the menu/UI layer still owns focus, a GB
# directional input can open the Joypad setup dialog instead of moving the avatar. Clicking the
# emulator client area before GB keyboard input has been a reliable way to restore gameplay focus.
